\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces This figure shows an example reconnection configuration on the scale of the ion skin depth for collisionless reconnection. The green arrows show the direction of flow into the x-point. Electrons trapped in a flux tube far from the x-point (with distribution function in panel A) are convected towards a region of lower field causing the flux tube to widen vertically (panel B). To enforce quasineutrality this requires the to shorten in the horizontal direction. The passing electrons (in green and purple which originate from a Maxwellian distribution) move to higher $v_\parallel $ whereas trapped electrons get smeared out causing the distribution to have an increasingly large $T_\parallel $ and smaller $T_\perp $.}}{6}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces The left panel shows an example reconnection region with a single field line identified in red. The guide field is in the $\phi $ direction. For low guide field, the field line has a very small radius of curvature, $R_B$, since the field line is sharply bent and $\rho _e \gg R_B$. In this case the electron does not conserve magnetic moment and the distribution function is isotropized. In the case of high guide field, the electron gyroradius is small relative to the radius of curvature and the pressure anisotropy is conserved.}}{8}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces Simulated kinetic reconnection with changing guide field reproduced from \cite {Le_Egedal_Ohia_Daughton_Karimabadi_Lukin_2013}. The top four panels, (a - d), show results from a full mass ratio PIC simulation of reconnection with a guide field of various amounts in pink squares on left. At the bottom is a fluid simulation with a closure that allows for pressure anisotropy. With varying guide field, the outflow characteristics change where very low or no guide field leads to no jets, moderate field leads to jets that pierce the center of the outflow, and high guide field returns to an outflow similar to the low guide field case.}}{9}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces PIC simulations of reconnection from \cite {Le_Egedal_Ohia_Daughton_Karimabadi_Lukin_2013}. The top row shows a moderate guide field run and the bottom row a low guide field run. With sufficiently high field, the pressure anisotropy in panel (a) stays in the outflow as compared to the low guide field. This leads to a marginal firehose condition of order $0.5$ (panel (b)) in the outflow which supports sharply bent field lines and a current jet in the center of the outflow (panel (c)).}}{10}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces A CAD diagram of the Big Red Ball. The experiment starts with a background field generated by a Helmholtz coil (1) into which plasma is injected via a set of plasma guns (2). After some time, the plasma has equilibrated and then a reverse field is driven via either a set of individual coils or a drive cylinder (3). A reconnecting current layer travels radially inward from high radius to low, passing probes along the way giving us the ability to trace the plasma parameters from one inflow, into an outflow, and into the other inflow.}}{12}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Timeline of a single shot for TREX. The permanent magnets are just that, permanent, and thus always on. About four seconds before the start of the shot, the Helmholtz coil is turned on to allow the field to penetrate the three inch thick aluminum wall. Plasma is injected at 12 ms along with an optional toroidal field coil. The drive coils are then pulsed with a ringing current. The first quarter period is of interest to us where first a pressure wave travels inwards, followed by a shock, and finally the reconnection layer.}}{13}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.7}{\ignorespaces Panels from flux arrays of experiment happening.}}{14}{figure.caption.16}%
\addvspace {10pt}
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Experimental measurements from a single shot where the probes are at $z=0$. The drive cylinder at $r = 0.6$ m turns on at $t = 0$. This releases the magnetic pressure at high radii allowing the plasma to expand which leads to a pressure wavefront moving radially inwards (orange). Later, a shock (green) is formed in front of the reconnection layer (purple dashed) which moves radially inwards. Once the wavefront passes, the magnetic field lines start moving outwards following the flux contours (black).}}{17}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Pressure Wave Observations in Bdot Measurements}}{18}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Pressure Wave Observations in Simulations}}{20}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces The simulated wavefront from a 1D linearized MHD simulation. Panel a shows the initial density profile and panel b shows the Alfv\'en speed (red) and fast magnetosonic speed (orange dashed) which are nearly the same. Panel c is the boundary velocity set by the applied loop voltage. Panel d is the velocity of the plasma which only begins to move outward once the wavefront passes with the different speed streamlines drawn on top. Panel e shows the expected measurements from a B-dot coil and flux contours which are similar to \ref {fig:experimental-evolution}, a.}}{30}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Traces from the flux array showing what the raw Bdot traces look like.}}{32}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces CAD rendering of BRB w/ drive cylinder, flux probes, and Te probe.}}{36}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.7}{\ignorespaces Plot of langmuir density and wave density. The minimum probe density is described in \ref {sss:multitip_min_density}.}}{36}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.8}{\ignorespaces The left plot shows the expected magnetic field evolution from the simulation and the right plot shows the evolution of $\partial _t B_z$ in time for a variety of radii which are in positions similar to figure \ref {fig:pressure_wave_raw_bdot_traces}. The orange line shows the wavefront with orange dots being the points where the wave crosses the radial position. The right panel shows the growth of high frequency modes in the wavefront.}}{38}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.9}{\ignorespaces The phase and group velocities for the fast magnetosonic wave as a function of frequency. The MHD cutoff is set to when the phase velocity differs by more than 10\% of the fast magnetosonic speed derived from MHD. The plasma parameters here are $n = 10^{18}$ m$^{-3}$, $B = 5$ mT, $T_e = 5$ eV, $T_i = 1$ eV, and propogation angle $\theta = 90^\circ $.}}{39}{figure.caption.27}%
